{"authored" [{"question" "q-89880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.$ Given that the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,2)}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & ={(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{(y)^{′}{x}^{2}-y({x}^{2})^{′}}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-2xy}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-2xy}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}-2xy)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-2xy}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,2)} & ={\\frac{y(1-2x)}{{x}^{4}}|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-2(1))}{(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,e)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,e),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {e}^{x}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(y{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}+e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =2e \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)}=e\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,e)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(0,e),$ therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,e).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of those listed"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of those listed"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-164138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-164138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))}{{(1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)}=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$ Therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-164138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-164138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-164138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course